.TH YES 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yes \- output a string repeatedly until killed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yes
.RI [ string ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yes
writes a line to standard output over and over.
The line consists of the
.I string
operands separated by single spaces, or
.B y
if there are none.
No options are recognised; every argument is output literally.
.PP
.B yes
stops when a write to standard output fails, for example with
.B EPIPE
when the reading end of a pipe has been closed and
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B yes
runs until killed by a signal, or exits with status
.B 1
when standard output can no longer be written.
.SH EXAMPLE
Answer every prompt of a command with
.BR y :
.EX
$ yes | rm \-i *.tmp
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR true (1)
